-- AWS SAA-C03 questions. Domains 1 Secure (30%), 2 Resilient (26%),
-- 3 High-Performing (24%), 4 Cost-Optimized (20%).
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- Domain 1: Secure Architectures
('sa-q-001','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$An application on EC2 needs to read from an S3 bucket. What is the MOST secure way to grant access?$q$,$q$["Store IAM access keys in the app config","Attach an IAM role to the EC2 instance","Make the bucket public","Embed root credentials in the AMI"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An instance role delivers automatically-rotated temporary credentials via metadata - no long-lived secrets on disk. Stored keys leak, public buckets expose data, and root credentials should never be used.$q$),
('sa-q-002','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which statement about security groups is correct?$q$,$q$["They are stateless and support deny rules","They are stateful and allow-only at the instance level","They operate at the subnet level","They filter Layer 7 HTTP content"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Security groups are stateful (return traffic auto-allowed), allow-only, and attached at the instance/ENI level. Network ACLs are the stateless, subnet-level, allow/deny control; WAF handles Layer 7.$q$),
('sa-q-003','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A company must apply a permission ceiling across all accounts in AWS Organizations so no account can disable CloudTrail. What should they use?$q$,$q$["An IAM user policy","A Service Control Policy (SCP)","A security group","A bucket policy"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SCPs set the maximum permissions for member accounts/OUs - a guardrail that can prevent even admins from disabling CloudTrail. IAM policies, security groups, and bucket policies don't span the org as ceilings.$q$),
('sa-q-004','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$How can you ensure all objects uploaded to an S3 bucket are encrypted at rest with auditable, rotatable keys?$q$,$q$["Enable SSE-KMS with a KMS key and enforce via bucket policy","Rely on HTTPS only","Use a public bucket","Disable versioning"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SSE-KMS encrypts at rest using KMS keys that support rotation and CloudTrail-audited usage; a bucket policy can deny unencrypted PUTs. HTTPS protects transit, not rest; public buckets and versioning are unrelated.$q$),
('sa-q-005','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',3,$q$A bucket policy should block any access that is not over HTTPS. Which condition achieves this?$q$,$q$["Deny when aws:SecureTransport is false","Allow when s3:x-amz-acl is public","Deny when aws:username is set","Allow all GET requests"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Denying requests where aws:SecureTransport is false forces TLS for all bucket access. The other conditions don't enforce encryption in transit.$q$),
('sa-q-006','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$What is envelope encryption as used by AWS KMS?$q$,$q$["Encrypting data twice with the same key","Encrypting data with a data key, which is itself encrypted by a KMS key","Storing keys in plaintext","Using only client-side hashing"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Envelope encryption encrypts data with a data key, then encrypts that data key with a KMS key; only the encrypted data key is stored, and KMS decrypts it on demand. This scales encryption without exposing the master key.$q$),
('sa-q-007','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$How should the AWS account root user be handled?$q$,$q$["Use it for daily administration","Enable MFA and lock it away, using IAM roles/users instead","Share it with the whole team","Attach a restrictive IAM policy to it"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Root should have MFA enabled and be reserved for the rare tasks only it can do; daily work uses least-privilege IAM identities. IAM policies cannot restrict root, so it must be protected, not constrained.$q$),
('sa-q-008','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$An RDS database must never be reachable from the internet. Which design achieves this?$q$,$q$["Place it in a public subnet with an EIP","Place it in a private subnet with a security group allowing only the app tier","Open its security group to 0.0.0.0/0","Assign it a public IP with SSL"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A private subnet with no public IP plus a security group referencing only the application tier keeps the database internal. Public subnets, elastic IPs, and 0.0.0.0/0 rules all expose it.$q$),
('sa-q-009','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Which service stores database credentials and rotates them automatically?$q$,$q$["S3","AWS Secrets Manager","CloudFront","Route 53"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Secrets Manager securely stores secrets like DB credentials with fine-grained IAM access and built-in automatic rotation. S3 stores objects, CloudFront is a CDN, Route 53 is DNS.$q$),
('sa-q-010','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',3,$q$An EC2 instance in a private subnet must reach S3 without traversing the public internet or a NAT Gateway. What should be used?$q$,$q$["An Internet Gateway","An S3 Gateway VPC endpoint","A public IP","A second NAT Gateway"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A Gateway VPC endpoint for S3 provides private connectivity within the VPC, avoiding both the internet and NAT Gateway data charges. Internet gateways and public IPs expose traffic; a second NAT still incurs cost.$q$),
('sa-q-011','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$What does AWS WAF protect against?$q$,$q$["Physical data center intrusion","Layer 7 web exploits like SQL injection and XSS","EBS volume failure","Root account misuse"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$AWS WAF filters Layer 7 web traffic (behind CloudFront, ALB, or API Gateway) against SQLi, XSS, bad bots, and floods. Physical security is AWS's job; EBS and root issues are unrelated.$q$),
('sa-q-012','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A partner in a separate AWS account needs temporary, auditable access to a specific S3 bucket. What is the BEST approach?$q$,$q$["Email them your access keys","Create a cross-account IAM role they assume via STS","Make the bucket public","Create a permanent IAM user for them with a password"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A cross-account role assumed via STS grants temporary, revocable, logged access without sharing credentials. Sharing keys, public buckets, and permanent external users are all insecure alternatives.$q$),
('sa-q-013','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Which service provides free, auto-renewing TLS certificates for an Application Load Balancer?$q$,$q$["KMS","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","Secrets Manager","IAM"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$ACM issues and auto-renews public TLS certificates integrated with ELB, CloudFront, and API Gateway. KMS manages encryption keys, Secrets Manager holds secrets, IAM handles access.$q$),
('sa-q-014','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A mobile app needs user sign-up/sign-in and then temporary AWS credentials to access resources. Which service fits?$q$,$q$["Amazon Cognito","AWS Config","CloudTrail","AWS Batch"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Cognito user pools handle sign-up/sign-in; identity pools vend temporary AWS credentials to authenticated users. Config tracks resource state, CloudTrail logs API calls, Batch runs batch jobs.$q$),
('sa-q-015','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which layered pair provides both instance-level and subnet-level network filtering?$q$,$q$["Security groups and Network ACLs","IAM and KMS","Route 53 and CloudFront","SQS and SNS"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Security groups filter at the instance level (stateful, allow-only) and NACLs at the subnet level (stateless, allow/deny) - complementary layers of defense. The other pairs are identity, DNS/CDN, and messaging.$q$),
('sa-q-016','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',3,$q$A security review finds an EC2 instance role with AdministratorAccess used only to read one DynamoDB table. Which principle is violated and the fix?$q$,$q$["Defense in depth; add a NACL","Least privilege; scope the policy to read-only on that table","High availability; add an AZ","Encryption; enable KMS"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Granting full admin for a single read task violates least privilege; the fix is a narrowly scoped policy allowing only the needed DynamoDB read actions on that table. The other options address different concerns.$q$),
('sa-q-017','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$What is the difference between SSE-S3 and SSE-KMS?$q$,$q$["SSE-S3 uses customer hardware; SSE-KMS uses no keys","SSE-S3 uses AWS-managed keys; SSE-KMS uses KMS keys with rotation and audit control","They are identical","SSE-KMS does not encrypt at rest"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SSE-S3 encrypts with keys AWS fully manages; SSE-KMS uses KMS keys giving you rotation policies, access control, and CloudTrail audit of key usage. Both encrypt at rest.$q$),
('sa-q-018','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Which AWS service protects against DDoS attacks at no additional cost, automatically?$q$,$q$["AWS Shield Standard","AWS Config","Amazon Inspector","AWS Batch"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Shield Standard is free and automatically defends against common network/transport DDoS. Shield Advanced adds paid Layer 7 protections. Config, Inspector, and Batch serve unrelated functions.$q$),
('sa-q-019','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the correct relationship between an S3 bucket policy and an IAM policy?$q$,$q$["Only one can exist at a time","Bucket policies are resource-based and can grant cross-account access; IAM policies are identity-based; both are evaluated","IAM policies are attached to buckets","Bucket policies override IAM entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Bucket policies attach to the resource (and can grant cross-account access); IAM policies attach to identities. AWS evaluates both, and access requires an allow with no explicit deny.$q$),
('sa-q-020','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Which service logs every use of a KMS key for auditing?$q$,$q$["CloudTrail","CloudFront","SQS","Route 53"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$CloudTrail records KMS API calls (encrypt, decrypt, key management) for audit and compliance. CloudFront is a CDN, SQS is queuing, Route 53 is DNS.$q$),
('sa-q-021','awssaa',1,'1.1','matching',2,$q$Match each AWS security service to its purpose.$q$,$q${"left":["IAM","KMS","WAF","Shield"],"right":["Authentication and authorization","Encryption key management","Layer 7 web exploit filtering","DDoS protection"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$IAM controls who can do what; KMS manages encryption keys; WAF filters Layer 7 web exploits; Shield defends against DDoS. Knowing each service's job is core to Domain 1.$q$),
-- Domain 2: Resilient Architectures
('sa-q-022','awssaa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$How do you make a web tier highly available against the failure of a single data center?$q$,$q$["Run all instances in one AZ","Distribute instances across multiple AZs behind a load balancer","Use a larger instance type","Disable health checks"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Spreading instances across multiple AZs behind an ELB means one AZ's failure doesn't take down the tier. A bigger instance in one AZ still has a single point of failure, and disabling health checks removes failover.$q$),
('sa-q-023','awssaa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$What does an RDS Multi-AZ deployment provide?$q$,$q$["Read scaling across regions","Automatic synchronous failover to a standby in another AZ","Cheaper storage","Global active-active writes"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Multi-AZ maintains a synchronous standby in another AZ and fails over automatically on primary failure - for availability, not read scaling (the standby isn't readable). Read replicas handle read scaling.$q$),
('sa-q-024','awssaa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$A stateless web app must automatically replace failed instances and scale with load. Which service does this?$q$,$q$["Auto Scaling Group","Route 53","KMS","CloudTrail"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An Auto Scaling Group maintains desired capacity across AZs, replaces unhealthy instances, and scales on demand - self-healing. Route 53 is DNS, KMS is keys, CloudTrail is auditing.$q$),
('sa-q-025','awssaa',2,'2.1','mcq',3,$q$An order-processing system must not lose requests when the processing tier is overwhelmed. Which design is BEST?$q$,$q$["Direct synchronous calls to the processing tier","Place an SQS queue between the web tier and processing tier","Store orders in instance memory","Use a single large instance"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An SQS queue decouples the tiers and buffers requests; if processing lags or fails, messages persist until capacity returns - nothing is lost. Synchronous calls and in-memory storage lose work on failure.$q$),
('sa-q-026','awssaa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$Which S3 feature lets you recover an object after an accidental overwrite or delete?$q$,$q$["Versioning","Transfer Acceleration","Requester Pays","Static website hosting"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$S3 Versioning retains previous versions and turns deletes into recoverable delete markers, protecting against accidental overwrites/deletes. The other features address transfer speed, billing, and hosting.$q$),
('sa-q-027','awssaa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$What is Amazon S3''s designed durability?$q$,$q$["99.9%","99.99%","Eleven nines (99.999999999%)","100% guaranteed forever"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$S3 is designed for 11 nines of durability by redundantly storing objects across multiple devices and AZs. The lower figures describe availability targets, not durability.$q$),
('sa-q-028','awssaa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$A workload requires DR in a second region with minimal data loss for object storage. What should you enable?$q$,$q$["S3 Cross-Region Replication","EBS encryption","A larger instance","CloudFront"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$S3 Cross-Region Replication asynchronously copies objects to a bucket in another region for DR/compliance (requires versioning). EBS encryption, instance sizing, and CloudFront don't provide cross-region object DR.$q$),
('sa-q-029','awssaa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$How can Route 53 automatically route users away from an unhealthy region?$q$,$q$["It cannot","Using health checks with failover routing","By resizing instances","By enabling versioning"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Route 53 health checks combined with failover (or latency) routing detect an unhealthy endpoint and direct DNS responses to a healthy one. Instance sizing and versioning are unrelated to DNS failover.$q$),
('sa-q-030','awssaa',2,'2.2','ordering',2,$q$Order the DR strategies from lowest cost/slowest recovery to highest cost/fastest recovery.$q$,$q$["Backup & Restore","Pilot Light","Warm Standby","Multi-Site Active/Active"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Backup & Restore is cheapest and slowest; Pilot Light keeps core pieces ready; Warm Standby runs a scaled-down full stack; Multi-Site active/active runs full duplicate infrastructure for near-zero RTO/RPO at the highest cost.$q$),
('sa-q-031','awssaa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$Why should application instances be stateless in a resilient AWS design?$q$,$q$["To reduce security","So any instance can serve any request and be freely replaced or scaled","To avoid using load balancers","To force single-AZ deployment"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Stateless instances let Auto Scaling add, remove, or replace them freely and let load balancers route to any of them - session state lives in DynamoDB or ElastiCache. This is foundational to elasticity and failover.$q$),
('sa-q-032','awssaa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$What is the recommended way to back up EBS volumes?$q$,$q$["Copy files to the same volume","EBS snapshots (stored in S3, incremental), automated with AWS Backup","Rely on instance store","Disable backups to save cost"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$EBS snapshots are incremental, stored durably in S3 across AZs, and can be automated via AWS Backup or Data Lifecycle Manager. Same-volume copies and instance store don't survive volume/instance loss.$q$),
('sa-q-033','awssaa',2,'2.1','mcq',3,$q$A globally distributed app needs low-latency reads and writes in multiple regions with regional failover. Which database feature fits?$q$,$q$["RDS Multi-AZ","DynamoDB Global Tables","A single EC2-hosted database","EBS Multi-Attach"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$DynamoDB Global Tables provide multi-region active-active replication with local low-latency access and failover. RDS Multi-AZ is single-region availability; a single self-hosted DB and EBS Multi-Attach don't span regions.$q$),
('sa-q-034','awssaa',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$What does an Elastic Load Balancer do when an instance fails its health check?$q$,$q$["Terminates the whole ASG","Stops routing traffic to that instance and uses healthy targets","Increases the instance size","Deletes the load balancer"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The ELB detects unhealthy targets via health checks and routes only to healthy ones across AZs, removing failed instances from rotation. It doesn't terminate ASGs, resize, or delete itself.$q$),
('sa-q-035','awssaa',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$Which combination best decouples and buffers a spiky ingestion workload?$q$,$q$["Direct database writes from the web tier","SQS to queue requests, with consumers scaling via an ASG","A single large RDS instance","Storing data in instance memory"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SQS buffers bursts while an Auto Scaling consumer fleet drains the queue at a sustainable rate - absorbing spikes without dropping work. Direct writes and in-memory storage fail under spikes.$q$),
-- Domain 3: High-Performing Architectures
('sa-q-036','awssaa',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Users worldwide complain of slow load times for static content served from one region. What improves performance MOST?$q$,$q$["A bigger origin instance","Amazon CloudFront CDN caching at edge locations","Disabling TLS","A second NAT Gateway"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$CloudFront caches content at edge locations near users, cutting latency and offloading the origin. A bigger origin doesn't fix distance; disabling TLS is insecure; NAT is irrelevant to content delivery.$q$),
('sa-q-037','awssaa',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$A read-heavy application repeatedly queries the same data, overloading the database. What is the BEST fix?$q$,$q$["Add ElastiCache to serve hot data from memory","Increase the RDS backup frequency","Switch to Spot Instances","Disable Multi-AZ"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$ElastiCache (Redis/Memcached) serves frequently accessed data from memory in microseconds, offloading the database. Backup frequency, Spot pricing, and Multi-AZ don't address repeated read load.$q$),
('sa-q-038','awssaa',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Which database is BEST for a workload needing single-digit-millisecond latency at massive scale with key-value access?$q$,$q$["Amazon RDS MySQL","Amazon DynamoDB","A self-managed database on EC2","Amazon Redshift"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$DynamoDB delivers consistent single-digit-millisecond latency at any scale for key-value/document access, serverless. RDS suits relational queries, Redshift is for analytics/warehousing.$q$),
('sa-q-039','awssaa',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$How do you scale read throughput for a busy RDS database without changing the app''s write path?$q$,$q$["Add read replicas and direct reads to them","Enable Multi-AZ","Increase the backup retention","Use a NAT Gateway"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Read replicas asynchronously copy the primary and serve read traffic, scaling reads horizontally. Multi-AZ is for failover (standby not readable); backups and NAT don't scale reads.$q$),
('sa-q-040','awssaa',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Which compute option runs event-driven code with automatic scaling and no servers to manage?$q$,$q$["EC2 On-Demand","AWS Lambda","Dedicated Hosts","EC2 Spot"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Lambda runs code in response to events, scaling automatically with per-invocation billing and no server management. All EC2 options require you to manage instances.$q$),
('sa-q-041','awssaa',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$A relational database needs consistently high IOPS with low latency. Which EBS volume type is BEST?$q$,$q$["Throughput-optimized HDD (st1)","Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2)","Cold HDD (sc1)","Instance store"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Provisioned IOPS SSD (io1/io2) delivers consistent high IOPS and low latency for demanding databases. HDD types suit sequential throughput, and instance store is ephemeral.$q$),
('sa-q-042','awssaa',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Which load balancer should terminate HTTPS and route based on URL path?$q$,$q$["Network Load Balancer","Application Load Balancer","Classic Load Balancer at Layer 4","A NAT Gateway"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The ALB operates at Layer 7 with host/path-based routing and TLS termination. NLB is Layer 4 for ultra-low latency; NAT Gateways aren't load balancers.$q$),
('sa-q-043','awssaa',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$An application ingests millions of clickstream events per second for real-time processing. Which service fits BEST?$q$,$q$["Amazon Kinesis","Amazon SQS standard queue","Amazon RDS","AWS Config"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Kinesis is built for high-throughput, ordered, replayable real-time streaming (clickstreams, telemetry). SQS is per-message queuing without ordered stream replay, RDS is relational, Config tracks resources.$q$),
('sa-q-044','awssaa',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Which EC2 placement group minimizes network latency between instances for HPC?$q$,$q$["Spread placement group","Cluster placement group","Partition placement group","No placement group"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A cluster placement group packs instances close together in one AZ for the lowest latency and highest throughput - ideal for HPC. Spread and partition prioritize resilience/distribution over latency.$q$),
('sa-q-045','awssaa',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$Which service accelerates long-distance uploads to S3 using the AWS edge network?$q$,$q$["S3 Transfer Acceleration","S3 Versioning","EBS snapshots","Route 53"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$S3 Transfer Acceleration routes uploads through CloudFront edge locations over the AWS backbone, speeding long-distance transfers. Versioning, snapshots, and DNS don't accelerate uploads.$q$),
('sa-q-046','awssaa',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$What performance advantage does Amazon Aurora have over standard RDS?$q$,$q$["It cannot scale","A distributed auto-scaling storage layer and up to 15 read replicas for higher throughput","It only supports Oracle","It has no replicas"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Aurora's distributed storage layer and up to 15 low-latency read replicas deliver much higher throughput than standard RDS while staying MySQL/PostgreSQL-compatible.$q$),
('sa-q-047','awssaa',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$How does Auto Scaling maintain performance under rising load?$q$,$q$["It deletes instances","It adds capacity via target tracking so response times stay consistent","It disables the load balancer","It reduces IOPS"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Auto Scaling adds instances as a tracked metric (e.g., CPU) rises, keeping performance steady under load, then scales in when demand falls. It doesn't remove needed capacity or disable the LB during growth.$q$),
('sa-q-048','awssaa',3,'3.2','mcq',3,$q$A workload needs a load balancer handling millions of TCP requests per second with ultra-low latency and a static IP. Which fits?$q$,$q$["Application Load Balancer","Network Load Balancer","Gateway per instance","CloudFront only"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The NLB operates at Layer 4, handling millions of requests per second at ultra-low latency with static IP support. The ALB is Layer 7 (feature-rich but higher latency); CloudFront is a CDN.$q$),
-- Domain 4: Cost-Optimized Architectures
('sa-q-049','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$A fault-tolerant batch job runs nightly and can be interrupted. Which pricing option is MOST cost-effective?$q$,$q$["On-Demand","Spot Instances","Dedicated Hosts","Reserved Instances (3-year)"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Spot offers up to 90% savings for interruptible, flexible workloads like nightly batch jobs. On-Demand is pricier, Dedicated Hosts are for compliance, and a 3-year RI over-commits for an intermittent job.$q$),
('sa-q-050','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$A predictable production workload will run 24/7 for three years. Which option minimizes cost?$q$,$q$["On-Demand","Spot","A Compute Savings Plan or Reserved Instances","Dedicated Hosts"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Steady, long-running workloads are cheapest on a 1-3 year Savings Plan or Reserved Instances (large discount vs On-Demand). Spot risks interruption for an always-on service; Dedicated Hosts cost more.$q$),
('sa-q-051','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Log data must be kept for compliance but is rarely accessed and can tolerate hours-long retrieval. Which storage class is cheapest?$q$,$q$["S3 Standard","S3 Standard-IA","S3 Glacier Deep Archive","S3 Intelligent-Tiering"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Glacier Deep Archive is the lowest-cost class for rarely accessed archives with hours-long retrieval - ideal for compliance logs. Standard and IA cost more; Intelligent-Tiering suits unpredictable access.$q$),
('sa-q-052','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Access patterns for a dataset are unpredictable and you want automatic cost optimization without retrieval fees. Which S3 class fits?$q$,$q$["S3 Standard","S3 Intelligent-Tiering","S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval","S3 One Zone-IA"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves objects between access tiers based on usage with no retrieval fees or performance impact - built for unpredictable access. The others require you to predict the pattern.$q$),
('sa-q-053','awssaa',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$How can you automatically move S3 objects to cheaper storage as they age?$q$,$q$["Manually copy them monthly","An S3 Lifecycle policy","Cross-Region Replication","Transfer Acceleration"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Lifecycle policies automatically transition objects to cheaper classes (or expire them) on a schedule - e.g., to Glacier after 30 days. Manual copying doesn't scale; replication and acceleration serve other goals.$q$),
('sa-q-054','awssaa',4,'4.2','mcq',3,$q$A NAT Gateway bill is high due to large volumes of S3 traffic from private instances. What reduces this cost?$q$,$q$["Add another NAT Gateway","Use an S3 Gateway VPC endpoint so S3 traffic bypasses the NAT","Enable versioning","Increase instance size"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A Gateway VPC endpoint routes S3 traffic privately within the VPC, bypassing the NAT Gateway's per-GB data-processing charges. Adding NATs increases cost; versioning and sizing don't affect NAT data charges.$q$),
('sa-q-055','awssaa',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$Which tool analyzes and forecasts AWS spending and can alert on budget thresholds?$q$,$q$["Cost Explorer and AWS Budgets","CloudTrail","Inspector","KMS"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Cost Explorer visualizes and forecasts spend, while AWS Budgets alerts when cost/usage crosses thresholds. CloudTrail logs API calls, Inspector assesses vulnerabilities, KMS manages keys.$q$),
('sa-q-056','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$How does right-sizing reduce cost?$q$,$q$["By always choosing the largest instance","By matching instance and volume sizes to actual utilization","By deleting all backups","By disabling Auto Scaling"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Right-sizing matches resources to real usage (using CloudWatch/Compute Optimizer), eliminating spend on idle over-provisioned capacity. Oversizing and disabling scaling increase cost.$q$),
('sa-q-057','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$For a spiky, event-driven API with long idle periods, which is MOST cost-effective?$q$,$q$["A 24/7 EC2 instance","AWS Lambda (pay per invocation)","A Dedicated Host","A large Reserved Instance"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Lambda charges only for actual executions, so idle periods cost nothing - ideal for spiky, event-driven workloads. Always-on EC2, Dedicated Hosts, and RIs pay for idle time.$q$),
('sa-q-058','awssaa',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$How does an Auto Scaling Group help control cost, not just performance?$q$,$q$["It never removes instances","It scales in during low demand so you stop paying for idle capacity","It forces On-Demand pricing","It disables health checks"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Auto Scaling removes instances when demand drops, so you pay only for the capacity you actually need, then adds it back as load rises - directly matching spend to demand.$q$),
('sa-q-059','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the difference between a Savings Plan and a Reserved Instance?$q$,$q$["Savings Plans commit to a dollar/hour spend for flexibility; RIs commit to specific instance attributes","They are identical","RIs offer no discount","Savings Plans only apply to S3"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Savings Plans commit to a consistent hourly compute spend and apply flexibly across families/regions; RIs commit to specific instance attributes. Both discount vs On-Demand over a 1-3 year term.$q$),
('sa-q-060','awssaa',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Data is accessed infrequently but must be retrieved within milliseconds when needed. Which class balances cost and access?$q$,$q$["S3 Standard-IA","S3 Glacier Deep Archive","S3 Standard","EBS io2"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Standard-IA offers lower storage cost for infrequently accessed data with millisecond retrieval (plus a retrieval fee). Deep Archive has hours-long retrieval, Standard costs more, and io2 is block storage, not object storage.$q$);
